 { This ancient study has attracted innumerable chess fans over the years. White seems completely lost, yet by choosing an optimal route for his king the game can be saved. } 1. { -KEY- } Kg7 $1 ( 1. Kh7 $2 h4 2. Kg6 h3 { would be useless. } ) 
h4 2. Kf6 Kb6 ( { After } 2... h3 3. Ke7 h2 ( { or } 3... Kb6 4. Kd6 ) 
4. c7 Kb7 5. Kd7 { both players queen. } ) 
3. Ke5 $1 ( { The key move, with the double intention of } 4. Kd6 { , assisting the c-pawn, and } 4. Kf4 { (or Ke4 ), catching the enemy pawn. } ) 
Kxc6 ( { Or } 3... h3 4. Kd6 h2 5. c7 Kb7 6. Kd7 ) 
4. Kf4 { Draw. Note the king's affinity with diagonal walks' - we will soon see more of this in the present section. } 
