 { The king's route is not an exclusive theme of pawn endings. Here are two examples from other types of endings. Apart from being a pawn down, White has to look watch out for the opponent's plan of Kh5-h4xh3 followed by the advance of the f-pawn. } 1. { -KEY- } Ke2 $1 { On its way to f3 the king steps on a light square, which is not a bad general recommendation when the opponent has a bishop of the other colour. } ( { In contrast, the game saw } 1. Ke3 $2 Bf6 $1 { and White had to resign immediately, in view of 2...Bg5(+) on the next move, with a decisive bishop exchange. } ) 
 ( { Another idea is } 1. b3 $5 Kh5 2. Ke2 Kh4 3. Kf3 $1 ( { king first; not } 3. Bxh6 Kg3 $1 ) 
f4 4. Kg2 $1 { with drawing chances. } ) 
Kh5 ( { Now if } 1... Bf6 { White has time for } 2. Bf4 $1 { intending } Bxb2 $6 3. Bxd6 Bd4 4. Kf3 ) 
2. Kf3 Bf6 3. Bf4 { White defends. } 
