 { A quick glance reveals that White will soon have to sacrifice his rook for the black d-pawn, after which the location of both kings will decide the outcome of the game. } 1. { -KEY- } Kc8 $1 { The king approaches the opposite flank without obstructing the rook. } ( { In contrast, after } 1. Kc6 $6 d2 2. Rd7 g5 $1 { gaining space; } ( 2. Kc8 Ke2 { loses to } 3. f4 $1 d1=Q $11 4. Rxd1 Kxd1 5. Kd7 Ke2 6. Ke7 Kf3 7. f5 { etc. } ) 
3. Rd5 Ke2 4. Kd6 d1=Q $11 5. Rxd1 Kxd1 6. Ke5 Ke2 7. Kf5 Kxf3 8. Kxg5 Ke4 $1 9. h4 Ke5 10. h5 f6 11. Kh6 f5 12. g5 f4 13. Kxh7 f3 14. g6 f2 15. g7 f1=Q $11 16. g8=Q $11 Kd6 { it is a theoretical draw, although White would have some winning chances in practice - Baburin. } ) 
f5 { Trying to gain some space before queening the pawn. } ( { After } 1... d2 2. Rd7 Ke2 3. f4 ( 2... f5 2. Kd8 { also works } ) 
d1=Q $11 4. Rxd1 Kxd1 5. f5 { followed by Kd7-e7, White wins. } ) 
2. gxf5 gxf5 3. Rd7 { The right angle for the rook, so as to control Black's passed pawn from behind. Also, it is important to prevent ...Kxf3 when two passed pawns would seriously increase Black's chances. } f4 4. Kd8 $1 { The point behind 1.Kc8 is revealed: the king can approach the enemy pawns without getting in the way of the rook. } d2 5. Ke7 Kxf3 ( { Or } 5... Ke2 6. Kf6 d1=Q $11 7. Rxd1 Kxd1 8. Kf5 { - Baburin. } ) 
6. Rxd2 { Evidently, without the h-pawns on the board this would be a simple draw. Incidentally, when explaining the principle 'pawn exchanges favour the defender' (more about this later on in the book), such positions are quite useful as starting points. } Ke3 7. Rd6 f3 8. Re6+ Kd2 9. h4 ( { Avoiding the last pitfall, } 9. Kf6 $2 f2 $1 ) 
 ( { After } 9. h4 { Black resigned, in view of } f2 10. Rf6 Ke1 11. h5 f1=Q $11 12. Rxf1 Kxf1 13. Kf7 { etc. } ) 

 1-0
